<p class="govuk-body">Your sentence has an end date. This means that you spend some time in prison, which is called being in custody. Then you are released into the community before the end of your sentence. This is called being on licence.</p>
<p class="govuk-body">Your sentence includes some additional time you need to serve on licence after you leave prison. You must follow your licence conditions until your licence ends.</p>
<p class="govuk-body">Some people on probation move to a stage where they don’t need to go to in-person appointments. This is called Final Third and is the last part of your sentence. This does not apply to everyone. Your probation officer will tell you if this applies to you.</p>
<p class="govuk-body">If you enter the Final Third, your probation meetings will stop. You must still follow the rules of your licence until your sentence ends. You can still speak to your probation officer to access support, if you need it.</p>
